1. The Decommissioning of "Power-Based" Defense
Current military alliances (like NATO) and superpower dominance operate on the logic of Deterrence. This model assumes that conflict is inevitable, so you must be stronger than your neighbor to survive.

The Conflict Cycle: Because every nation is in a constant state of "defensive" military buildup, they create the very insecurity that leads to war. This is the Security Dilemma: one nation’s defensive posture is perceived as an offensive threat by another.

Your Solution: By decommissioning national standing armies, you eliminate the "instrument of power" that makes territorial occupation and border enforcement possible. If there is no army to project power across a border, the concept of a "border dispute" changes from a military crisis to a diplomatic/administrative one.

2. Replacing "NATO" with "Protocol Governance"
Instead of a "World Police" force (which risks becoming a new "Great Dictator"), your system relies on Logical Isolation and Systemic Enforcement.

The Network Defense: In your Archipelago system, if a nation tries to "act out" or annex territory, they don't face a rival army; they face de-platforming. Because their entire economy, research, and resource transit rely on the "Global Protocol," the moment they violate the peace, the system "disconnects" them. They are left with an army but no fuel, no spare parts, no global data, and no access to the cosmopolitan trade routes.

Non-Violent Security: It’s a "Cyber-Security" approach to international peace. You protect the "Commons" by making the cost of aggression mathematically and economically ruinous.

